# Rigor core overlay — supplemental core signatures.
#
# --- Proc#refined (Ruby 4.1) ---
#
# Ruby 4.1 adds `Proc#refined(*modules)` (Feature #22097, `proc_refined` in
# CRuby's `proc.c`): it returns a copy of the receiver whose body runs with
# the given modules' refinements active. Upstream `ruby/rbs` does not declare
# it through 4.2, so
#
#   pr = ->(s) { s.shout }.refined(Shout)
#
# reported `call.undefined-method` on correct 4.1 code.
#
# The return is `self`, not `Proc`. CRuby builds the copy with
# `proc_create(rb_obj_class(self), …, is_lambda)` (`vm.c`'s
# `rb_proc_dup_with_iseq_and_recipe`) and returns the receiver itself when
# called with no modules, so the class, lambda-ness, `parameters`, `arity`
# and source location all carry over (`test_refined_preserves_lambda`,
# `test_refined_preserves_location_and_parameters` in `test/ruby/test_proc.rb`).
# A refinement changes which methods the body's calls reach, never the
# Proc's own signature.
#
# Not modelled: `refined` raises `ArgumentError` on a Proc not backed by a
# Ruby block (`:sym.to_proc`, `method(:m).to_proc`), and `TypeError` for a
# Class argument (`Check_Type(arg, T_MODULE)`), which `*Module` accepts.
#
# Unconditional, not gated on `target_ruby >= 4.1` (issue #1665): gating would
# report `call.undefined-method` on every correct 4.1 project still on the
# default `target_ruby`. False positives outrank the missed 4.0-target error.
#
# Declared on a module `Proc` includes, not with a direct `def`, for the reason
# `process.rbs` gives: a later rbs release or a project `sig/` is likely to
# declare `Proc#refined` itself, and a second direct `def` is a
# `DuplicatedMethodDefinitionError` that takes `Proc`'s whole instance surface
# down to `Dynamic[top]`. A direct `def` overrides an included module's method,
# so the overlay stands down instead of colliding.
module RigorCoreOverlay
  module ProcRefined
    def refined: (*Module) -> self
  end
end

class Proc
  include RigorCoreOverlay::ProcRefined
end
